---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="what-is-a-csv-column-picker">
    <h2>What is a csv column picker? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A csv column picker is a tool that keeps the columns you name and drops every other field.
    </p>
    <p>
      You paste a table, list the headers to keep, and read a smaller CSV back in that same order.
      The cut runs on your device, so a customer export never gets uploaded.
      People reach for it when a download is wider than the sheet they can share.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pull name and email out of a wide CRM export before a mail merge.
      Drop a salary column before sharing a spreadsheet with someone who should not see pay.
    </p>
    <p>The page is free to open, and there is no account to create.</p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try the CSV Column Picker →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-extract-columns">
    <h2>How do you extract columns from csv? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Paste the table in the first box and the columns to keep in the second.</p>
    <p>
      Separate names with commas, and keep the capitalization of each header exactly.
      The output follows the order you type rather than the order in the file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Detection works by counting commas against semicolons on the first non-empty line, then checking tabs.
      That choice is the delimiter for the whole file, including every later row.
    </p>
    <p>Input: the Ada row with name, age, city in that order.</p>
    <div class="pick-scroll">
      <table class="pick-sample">
        <caption>Sample: Ada lives in Pune and is 30.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">name</th>
            <th scope="col">age</th>
            <th scope="col">city</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Ada</td>
            <td>30</td>
            <td>Pune</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      For example, the list city,name on that table returns city before name.
      Typing 3 returns only the city column, and the notice calls the cut a position pick.
      Output: Pune stays under city, while the age column is gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Add Bob, 41, Goa under Ada and the same list returns a second row.
      Goa lines up under city, and 41 leaves with the age column.
    </p>
    <p>
      Leave the second box empty and the whole table comes back with the detected delimiter.
      The summary names the headers, so you can confirm the read before you drop a field.
    </p>
    <p>A second try is instant, because the cut updates as you type.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="name-or-position">
    <h2>Should you pick by header name or by position? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use a header name when the first row has labels, and use a column number only for positive integers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difference between them shows up when someone inserts a column.
      A position number is not a stable field name.
      Select csv columns by header so you keep csv columns after a new field appears.
      A name such as city still finds Pune after a phone field lands at the front.
      A number such as 3 then points at a different field, so the notice warns you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Picking by position after a column is inserted keeps the wrong field in a tidy looking grid.
      Unlike a name, a slot number does not know what the field means.
      Use names on every file that already has a header row.
    </p>
    <p>
      A mixed list such as city,3 is treated as names, so a column literally called 3 would have to exist.
      Numbers count as positions only when every entry is a positive integer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="semicolon-files">
    <h2>Why does a semicolon file collapse to one column? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>It collapses because a comma split of name;age sees one column and the picker switches.</p>
    <p>
      The notice says the file was read as semicolon because a comma split it into one column.
      A semicolon csv is that European export, reread so the headers become columns you can name.
      Treating a semicolon file as comma-separated is how a European export becomes one smashed field.
      You do not pick the delimiter yourself, and the same check applies to every row.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tabs win when a tab split produces more columns than a comma split.
      Keep city and name from a European semicolon export by typing those headers once the notice is up.
    </p>
    <p>
      Quoted commas do not count as splits, because the parser honors quotes before it cuts.
      In a note cell that holds hello, world, keeping note preserves the comma inside the cell.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="missing-header">
    <h2>What happens when a header is missing? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>It stops and names the missing header instead of writing a partial file.</p>
    <p>
      For example, email against name and age stops the run and names email.
      Check the spelling and the capitalization, because Name does not match name.
    </p>
    <p>
      A repeated header is a different problem, not a missing name.
      The first match is the column that stays in the output.
      A notice says a later column with the same name was left behind.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ignoring a missing header and guessing the column would hide a typo until a later join fails.
      Correct the list and run the cut again instead of filling the gap by hand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="read-the-notice">
    <h2>How do you read the notice? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Read the notice when it appears, because a quiet result can still be the wrong cut.</p>
    <p>
      An ordinary comma file with a name list has no notice at all.
      A semicolon or tab file always explains why the delimiter changed.
      A position list always warns that inserting a column changes which field is kept.
    </p>
    <p>
      Names do not set that position warning, even if you reorder the columns.
      If two headers share a name, the notice names the duplicate that was left behind.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cell-values">
    <h2>Does the picker change cell values? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>The picker does not edit cell values, so a date and a leading zero stay as written.</p>
    <p>
      Quotes come back only when the cell contains the delimiter or a line break.
      A short row gains blank cells rather than borrowing a value from the next column.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="before-and-after">
    <h2>What should you run before and after the cut? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Clean ragged rows first, then cut columns, then change format only if you need to.</p>
    <p>
      Run the <a href={toolPathBySlug('csv-cleaner')}>CSV Cleaner</a> when rows disagree about how many fields they hold.
      After the cut, the <a href={toolPathBySlug('csv-to-json-converter')}>CSV to JSON converter</a> turns the smaller file into objects for an API.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the smaller file should be tab separated, send it through the
      <a href={toolPathBySlug('csv-to-tsv')}>CSV to TSV converter</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>What are the common mistakes? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Each common mistake below is a cut that looks finished and keeps the wrong field.</p>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Why is a column number risky?">
      <p>Picking by position after a column is inserted keeps a different field.</p>
      <p>Read the notice, and switch to header names before you share the file.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="What if you split a semicolon export on commas?">
      <p>Treating a semicolon file as comma-separated collapses every row into one cell.</p>
      <p>This picker switches the delimiter and says so in the notice when that happens.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="What if you guess past a missing header?">
      <p>Ignoring a missing header and guessing the column hides the typo.</p>
      <p>This picker names the missing header and stops, so you can correct the list.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
  .pick-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0;
  }
  .pick-sample {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  .pick-sample caption {
    caption-side: bottom;
    text-align: start;
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
  .pick-sample th,
  .pick-sample td {
    text-align: start;
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .pick-sample th {
    background: var(--color-bg-tertiary);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }
</style>
